    "title": "Sophocles' Antigone: Law, Conscience and the State",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Greek tragedy",
    "passage": [
      "Sophocles' Antigone was first performed in Athens around 441 BCE. It follows the war between Oedipus' sons, Eteocles and Polynices, who killed each other in single combat at the gates of Thebes. Their uncle Creon, now king, gives Eteocles an honored burial and decrees that Polynices, who led a foreign army against the city, must be left unburied, on pain of death for anyone who buries him.",
      "Antigone, their sister, defies the decree. She says she obeys the unwritten and unfailing laws of the gods, which demand burial for the dead, and that no mortal's proclamation can override them. Her sister Ismene refuses to help, saying that women cannot fight men or the power of the state. Antigone is caught, admits the act openly, and is sentenced to be walled up alive in a tomb.",
      "Creon argues that a city cannot survive if traitors are honored like defenders and if rulers are disobeyed. He is challenged by his son Haemon, who is engaged to Antigone and reports that the city quietly sympathizes with her, and by the blind prophet Tiresias, who warns that the gods are offended by the unburied corpse. Creon relents too late: Antigone has hanged herself, Haemon kills himself beside her, and Creon's wife Eurydice kills herself on hearing the news.",
      "The play has been read in sharply different ways. The philosopher G. W. F. Hegel argued that it stages a collision between two legitimate claims, the family and divine law on one side and the state and human law on the other, each of which is one-sided. Others read Antigone as simply right and Creon as a tyrant, pointing out that the gods side with her. Still others stress Antigone's own harshness, her contempt for Ismene and her eagerness for death.",
      "Readers also dispute whose tragedy it is. Antigone dies in the middle of the action, while Creon survives to the end, having lost his son and wife and recognized his error. The chorus closes by saying that wisdom is the chief part of happiness and that the proud learn wisdom only through suffering.",
      "Modern writers have used the play to think about civil disobedience, resistance to occupation and the rights of the dead. Whether its conflict can be settled, and by what standard, remains open."
    ],
