    "title": "Crime and Punishment: Raskolnikov's Theory and Redemption",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Russian novel",
    "passage": [
      "Fyodor Dostoevsky's Crime and Punishment (1866) follows Rodion Raskolnikov, a poor former law student in St. Petersburg who murders an old pawnbroker, Alyona Ivanovna, with an axe. Her gentle half-sister Lizaveta walks in during the crime and he kills her too. He takes a few small items and hides them without ever using them.",
      "Before the murder Raskolnikov had published an article arguing that humanity divides into ordinary people, who must obey the law, and a few extraordinary people, such as Napoleon, who have the right to overstep moral limits if their ideas require it. He also rehearses a utilitarian argument he overhears in a tavern: killing one harmful old woman and using her money for many good deeds would be a net good.",
      "After the crime he falls into fever, paranoia and isolation. The investigator Porfiry Petrovich suspects him and plays a long psychological game, urging him to confess. Raskolnikov is drawn to Sonya Marmeladova, a devout young woman forced into prostitution to support her family, who reads him the story of the raising of Lazarus. He finally confesses to her and then to the police.",
      "He is sentenced to eight years of penal servitude in Siberia, and Sonya follows him. For much of the epilogue he still regrets mainly that he failed, not that he killed. Only near the end does he weep at her feet, and the narrator says a gradual renewal is beginning, a story for another time.",
      "Readers disagree about why he killed. Raskolnikov himself offers several motives: poverty, to help his mother and sister, to test whether he is extraordinary, and finally to admit that he killed for himself. Critics also dispute whether the novel refutes his theory by argument or only by his psychological collapse, and whether the religious epilogue is earned or tacked on.",
      "The book is often read as Dostoevsky's answer to rationalist and utilitarian ideas of his day, but it gives those ideas strong voices, and some readers find the counter-arguments of characters such as Svidrigailov more unsettling than the official resolution."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "motive", "title": "Explain why Raskolnikov killed", "description": "Defends an account of his real motive among the several he gives.",
        "concept": "motive", "definition": "The reason that actually moves a character to act, as distinct from the reasons he offers.",
        "facts": ["Raskolnikov is desperately poor and worried for his mother and sister.", "He tests whether he belongs to the extraordinary few.", "He never uses the stolen goods.", "He eventually tells Sonya he killed for himself.", "A strong answer explains why some stated motives are rationalizations."],
        "misconceptions": ["He killed simply for money.", "A character's own explanation is always the true one."] },
      { "key": "extraordinary-man", "title": "Assess the extraordinary man theory", "description": "Judges whether the novel refutes Raskolnikov's theory by argument or only shows that he personally could not live with it.",
        "concept": "extraordinary-man-theory", "definition": "Raskolnikov's claim that a few great people have the right to transgress moral law for the sake of their ideas.",
        "facts": ["His article divides people into ordinary and extraordinary.", "He invokes Napoleon as a model.", "His collapse after the murder is psychological and physical.", "Readers dispute whether his failure disproves the theory or only shows he was not extraordinary."],
        "misconceptions": ["If Raskolnikov suffers, the theory has been logically refuted.", "Great historical figures prove the theory true."] },
      { "key": "utilitarian-murder", "title": "Weigh the one-for-many argument", "description": "Evaluates the tavern argument that killing one harmful person to help many is justified.",
        "concept": "consequentialist-justification", "definition": "The view that an act is justified if its total good consequences outweigh its harms.",
        "facts": ["The overheard argument treats the pawnbroker as harmful and her money as able to do much good.", "The murder of Lizaveta was never part of the calculation.", "Raskolnikov does none of the promised good deeds.", "A strong answer separates the argument's logic from how it plays out in the novel."],
        "misconceptions": ["The ends always justify the means.", "Dostoevsky gives the argument no force at all."] },
      { "key": "redemption", "title": "Judge the epilogue's redemption", "description": "Argues whether Raskolnikov's renewal through Sonya is earned by the novel or imposed on it.",
        "concept": "redemption", "definition": "Moral and spiritual renewal after wrongdoing, here linked to suffering, love and faith.",
        "facts": ["Sonya reads him the raising of Lazarus.", "In Siberia he at first regrets only his failure.", "His change comes late and the narrator calls it only a beginning.", "Some critics find the ending unconvincing; others see it prepared throughout."],
        "misconceptions": ["Confession alone means he has repented.", "The novel ends with Raskolnikov fully transformed."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
      "ko": { "title": "죄와 벌: 라스콜니코프의 이론과 구원",
        "objectives": { "motive": "라스콜니코프가 살인한 이유 설명하기", "extraordinary-man": "비범인 이론 평가하기", "utilitarian-murder": "다수를 위한 한 사람의 희생 논리 따져 보기", "redemption": "에필로그의 구원 판단하기" } },
      "es": { "title": "Crimen y castigo: la teoría y la redención de Raskólnikov",
        "objectives": { "motive": "Explicar por qué mató Raskólnikov", "extraordinary-man": "Evaluar la teoría del hombre extraordinario", "utilitarian-murder": "Sopesar el argumento de uno por muchos", "redemption": "Juzgar la redención del epílogo" } }
