    "title": "Pride and Prejudice: Marriage, Judgment and Irony",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "English novel",
    "passage": [
      "Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice (1813) follows the Bennet family, whose five daughters cannot inherit their father's estate because it is entailed to a male cousin, Mr. Collins. Marriage is therefore a matter of economic survival as well as feeling. The famous opening sentence ironically states that a single man with a fortune must be in want of a wife, when the novel shows it is the families of poor young women who are in want.",
      "Elizabeth Bennet, the second daughter, is witty and confident in her judgments. At a ball she overhears the wealthy Mr. Darcy call her merely tolerable, and she forms a lasting dislike of him. She readily believes the charming officer Mr. Wickham, who claims Darcy cheated him of an inheritance.",
      "Darcy falls in love with Elizabeth despite himself and proposes, insulting her family and her social position in the same speech. She refuses him, accusing him of ruining her sister Jane's chances with his friend Bingley and of wronging Wickham. Darcy's letter in reply reveals that Wickham tried to elope with Darcy's fifteen-year-old sister for her fortune. Elizabeth realizes she has been blind and prejudiced and says that until this moment she never knew herself.",
      "Later Wickham runs off with Elizabeth's youngest sister, Lydia, threatening the whole family's reputation. Darcy secretly pays Wickham to marry her. Elizabeth, having seen Darcy's estate at Pemberley and his changed manners, accepts his second proposal. Earlier her friend Charlotte Lucas had accepted the absurd Mr. Collins for security, saying she was not romantic.",
      "Critics debate how far the novel endorses or questions its world. Some read it as a romance in which merit and love are rewarded. Others note that the happy ending depends on Darcy's enormous wealth and that Austen's irony exposes the economic pressures on women without overturning them. Readers also dispute whether the title's pride belongs to Darcy and the prejudice to Elizabeth, or whether both have both."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "first-impressions", "title": "Explain Elizabeth's misjudgment", "description": "Accounts for why a perceptive heroine misjudges Darcy and Wickham, and what her self-recognition means.",
        "concept": "judgment-and-prejudice", "definition": "Forming firm opinions of people from first impressions, wounded vanity or charm rather than evidence.",
        "facts": ["Elizabeth overhears Darcy's slight at the first ball.", "She believes Wickham's story without checking it.", "Darcy's letter reveals Wickham's attempt to elope with Georgiana.", "She says she never knew herself until then.", "A strong answer links her error to her own pride, not only to Darcy's faults."],
        "misconceptions": ["Elizabeth is always right about people.", "Darcy is entirely to blame for the misunderstanding."] },
      { "key": "pride-prejudice", "title": "Decide who is proud and who prejudiced", "description": "Argues whether the title divides the faults between Darcy and Elizabeth or whether each shares both.",
        "concept": "pride-and-prejudice", "definition": "The two faults named in the title and how they are distributed among the main characters.",
        "facts": ["Darcy's first proposal insults Elizabeth's family and status.", "Elizabeth's dislike begins with wounded vanity.", "Both characters change their behavior and views."],
        "misconceptions": ["Darcy is pride and Elizabeth is prejudice, and that is all."] },
      { "key": "charlotte", "title": "Judge Charlotte Lucas's marriage", "description": "Evaluates whether Charlotte's practical marriage to Mr. Collins is sensible, tragic or a criticism of her society.",
        "concept": "marriage-and-economics", "definition": "The way property law and women's lack of income made marriage an economic necessity.",
        "facts": ["The Bennet estate is entailed to Mr. Collins.", "Charlotte accepts Collins for security and says she is not romantic.", "Elizabeth is at first shocked by the choice.", "A defensible answer considers Charlotte's limited options."],
        "misconceptions": ["Charlotte is simply foolish or mercenary.", "Only marrying for love is ever reasonable in the novel's world."] },
      { "key": "irony-and-society", "title": "Assess whether the novel challenges its society", "description": "Argues whether Austen's irony criticizes the marriage market or ultimately endorses it through a fairy-tale ending.",
        "concept": "irony", "definition": "Saying one thing while meaning another, used here to expose the gap between social ideals and economic reality.",
        "facts": ["The opening sentence reverses who is really in want.", "The happy ending depends on Darcy's wealth.", "Lydia's elopement shows how fragile a family's reputation is.", "Critics differ on whether Austen is conservative or subversive."],
        "misconceptions": ["The novel is only a love story.", "Because the heroine marries rich, the novel must approve of the system."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
