    "title": "Nineteen Eighty-Four: Power, Truth and Resistance",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Dystopian novel",
    "passage": [
      "George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) is set in Oceania, a superstate ruled by the Party under the image of Big Brother. Winston Smith works at the Ministry of Truth, rewriting old newspaper articles so that the record always agrees with what the Party says now. Telescreens watch citizens in their homes, and the Thought Police punish even private doubt.",
      "The Party controls thought through language and memory. Newspeak shrinks the vocabulary so that rebellious ideas cannot be expressed. Doublethink is the ability to hold two contradictory beliefs and accept both. The Party's slogans declare that war is peace, freedom is slavery and ignorance is strength, and one of its principles holds that whoever controls the past controls the future.",
      "Winston begins a diary and a forbidden love affair with Julia, a younger woman who rebels for pleasure rather than from political theory. He writes that freedom is the freedom to say that two plus two make four. He believes hope lies with the proles, the poor majority the Party largely ignores. He and Julia approach O'Brien, an Inner Party member they believe belongs to a secret resistance.",
      "O'Brien is a loyal Party agent. Winston is arrested and tortured in the Ministry of Love. O'Brien tells him that the Party seeks power entirely for its own sake, and that reality exists only in the collective mind of the Party. In Room 101 Winston is faced with his worst fear, rats, and begs that it be done to Julia instead. Released, he no longer loves her, and the novel ends with him loving Big Brother.",
      "Readers argue about whether the book is a warning about a particular system, Soviet totalitarianism, or about tendencies in every modern state. They also debate whether it leaves any hope. The bleak ending suggests total defeat, but the novel closes with an appendix on Newspeak written in the past tense, which some read as a sign that the Party eventually fell."
    ],
    "objectives": [
