        "facts": ["Winston rewrites records at the Ministry of Truth.", "Newspeak narrows what can be thought.", "Doublethink lets people accept contradictions.", "O'Brien claims reality exists only in the Party's mind.", "Winston's two plus two makes four stands for objective truth."],
        "misconceptions": ["The Party rules only through violence.", "Propaganda works only on stupid people."] },
      { "key": "power-for-itself", "title": "Assess power pursued for its own sake", "description": "Judges O'Brien's claim that the Party wants power purely for itself, and whether that is believable.",
        "concept": "power-as-end", "definition": "The idea that a ruling group seeks domination as an end in itself rather than for any further good.",
        "facts": ["O'Brien rejects the idea that the Party rules for others' good.", "He describes the future as a boot stamping on a human face.", "Some readers find this candid account more frightening than ideological rule.", "A strong answer asks why the Party would admit its aim."],
        "misconceptions": ["All dictators sincerely believe they serve the people.", "Power is always a means to wealth."] },
