  {
    "title": "Brave New World: Happiness Against Freedom",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Dystopian novel",
    "passage": [
      "Aldous Huxley's Brave New World (1932) imagines a World State in which people are grown in hatcheries and conditioned before and after birth into fixed castes, from intelligent Alphas to Epsilons bred for simple labor. Each caste is taught to be content with its place. The motto of the state is community, identity, stability.",
      "Citizens are kept happy by constant consumption, casual sex without attachment, entertainment and soma, a drug that gives pleasure without hangover. Family, parenthood, deep love, art that disturbs and religion have been abolished as sources of instability. History is dismissed as bunk, a phrase attributed to Henry Ford, whom the state treats as a near-divine founder.",
      "Bernard Marx, an Alpha who feels out of place, brings back John, a young man raised on a savage reservation in New Mexico. John has learned his values from a battered volume of Shakespeare and from the reservation's mix of Native and Christian rituals. In London he is first a celebrity, then disgusted by what he sees.",
      "In a long debate, the World Controller Mustapha Mond argues that stability and happiness required giving up high art, science pursued freely and religion. John replies that he wants God, poetry, real danger, freedom, goodness and sin, and Mond notes that he is claiming the right to be unhappy. John withdraws to a lighthouse to live austerely, is mobbed by crowds who come to watch him whip himself, and hangs himself.",
      "Readers disagree about where Huxley stands. The World State is plainly meant to disturb, yet many of its people are content, and John is no clear alternative: his ideals are borrowed from books and end in self-punishment and despair. Huxley later wrote that he wished he had offered a third possibility between the two. The book also invites comparison with Nineteen Eighty-Four: control through pleasure rather than through fear."
    ],
