        "facts": ["Citizens are conditioned to like their place.", "Soma removes distress on demand.", "Art, religion and family were abolished for stability.", "Mond says John is claiming the right to be unhappy.", "A strong answer says why felt contentment might or might not be enough."],
        "misconceptions": ["Anyone in the World State is secretly miserable.", "More pleasure always means a better life."] },
      { "key": "mond-argument", "title": "Evaluate Mustapha Mond's defense", "description": "Assesses the Controller's argument that the sacrifices were necessary and worth it.",
        "concept": "benevolent-control", "definition": "Rule that limits people's freedom on the claim that it secures their well-being.",
        "facts": ["Mond has read the forbidden books and knows what was given up.", "He argues that stability requires suppressing truth and beauty that disturb.", "He chose to serve the state rather than pursue science freely.", "A defensible reply addresses his reasons, not only his conclusion."],
        "misconceptions": ["Mond is a simple villain who does not understand what he destroyed."] },
