  {
    "title": "Things Fall Apart: Okonkwo and Colonial Encounter",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "African novel",
    "passage": [
      "Chinua Achebe's Things Fall Apart (1958) is set among the Igbo villages of Umuofia in what is now southeastern Nigeria, in the late nineteenth century. Its protagonist Okonkwo rose from poverty to wealth and fame as a wrestler, farmer and warrior. He is driven by fear of being like his father, Unoka, a gentle debtor and musician whom the community considered a failure.",
      "Achebe shows Igbo society in detail: its councils, religion, proverbs, justice, festivals and debates. The community admires strength but also values restraint, and many characters question its customs. Okonkwo, fearing to look weak, joins in killing Ikemefuna, a boy who had lived in his household for three years and called him father, after the Oracle decrees his death. His friend Obierika had advised him to stay away.",
      "Okonkwo accidentally kills a clansman and is exiled for seven years to his mother's village, where his uncle Uchendu tells him that a man belongs to his motherland in times of sorrow. During this time Christian missionaries arrive, followed by a colonial administration with courts and soldiers. Okonkwo's sensitive son Nwoye, troubled by Ikemefuna's death and the abandonment of twins, joins the Christians.",
      "When Okonkwo returns, Umuofia is divided. After a confrontation, he and other leaders are jailed and humiliated. At a meeting he kills a court messenger, but the clan does not rise with him. He hangs himself, an act that his own people regard as an abomination. The novel ends with the District Commissioner planning a book in which Okonkwo's story will earn perhaps a paragraph.",
      "Achebe wrote partly in answer to European novels that portrayed Africans as without history or culture. Readers debate whether Okonkwo is a tragic hero brought down by his own flaw, a victim of colonialism, or a figure whose rigid idea of manhood already put him at odds with his own society. The title, from Yeats's poem The Second Coming, raises the question of what exactly falls apart and why."
    ],
