  {
    "title": "The Great Gatsby and the American Dream",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "American novel",
    "passage": [
      "F. Scott Fitzgerald's The Great Gatsby (1925) is narrated by Nick Carraway, a young Midwesterner who moves to West Egg on Long Island in the summer of 1922. His neighbor Jay Gatsby throws enormous parties and is the subject of wild rumors. Across the bay live Nick's cousin Daisy and her wealthy, brutal husband Tom Buchanan.",
      "Gatsby was born James Gatz to poor farm people in North Dakota and reinvented himself. As a young officer he fell in love with Daisy, but she married Tom while he was away. He made his fortune through dealings with the gambler Meyer Wolfsheim, apparently including bootlegging, and bought his mansion to be near Daisy. He stares at the green light at the end of her dock. When Nick suggests one cannot repeat the past, Gatsby insists that of course one can.",
      "Nick arranges a reunion, and Gatsby and Daisy begin an affair. In a confrontation at the Plaza Hotel, Tom exposes Gatsby's shady business and Daisy cannot say she never loved Tom. Driving back in Gatsby's car, Daisy strikes and kills Myrtle Wilson, Tom's mistress. Gatsby protects her. Tom lets Myrtle's husband George believe Gatsby was the driver, and George shoots Gatsby and then himself. Almost no one comes to the funeral.",
      "Nick calls Tom and Daisy careless people who smashed things and then retreated into their money. He also says Gatsby was worth the whole rotten crowd put together. Yet Nick is not a neutral witness: he is drawn into the events, he helped arrange the affair, and he calls himself one of the few honest people he has known.",
      "Readers dispute what the novel says about the American dream: whether Gatsby embodies its idealism, exposes its corruption by money, or shows that it was always an illusion about class mobility. They also argue whether Gatsby is a romantic hero, a criminal fraud or both, and whether Daisy is a shallow villain or a woman trapped by the choices open to her."
    ],
