    "title": "Macbeth: Ambition, Fate and Guilt",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Shakespearean tragedy",
    "passage": [
      "William Shakespeare's Macbeth (about 1606) opens with three witches and with reports of Macbeth's courage in battle for King Duncan of Scotland. The witches greet Macbeth as Thane of Glamis, Thane of Cawdor and future king, and tell his companion Banquo that his descendants will be kings. Almost at once Macbeth learns he has been made Thane of Cawdor.",
      "Macbeth thinks of murdering Duncan and is horrified by the thought. His wife, Lady Macbeth, reading his letter, fears he is too full of the milk of human kindness. She calls on spirits to unsex her and fill her with cruelty, and when he hesitates she attacks his manhood. Macbeth kills Duncan as he sleeps under their roof, and Lady Macbeth plants the daggers on the grooms.",
      "Macbeth becomes king but cannot rest. He has Banquo murdered, though Banquo's son Fleance escapes, and he sees Banquo's ghost at a feast. He returns to the witches, who show apparitions warning him of Macduff but promising that no man born of woman can harm him and that he is safe until Birnam Wood comes to Dunsinane. He has Macduff's wife and children slaughtered.",
      "Lady Macbeth sleepwalks, trying to wash invisible blood from her hands, and dies, apparently by suicide. Hearing of it, Macbeth reflects that life is a tale told by an idiot, signifying nothing. The prophecies turn out to be equivocations: Malcolm's soldiers carry boughs from Birnam Wood, and Macduff was delivered from his mother's womb untimely. Macduff kills Macbeth, and Malcolm becomes king.",
      "Critics debate how much the witches control events. Some read them as agents of fate; others note that they never tell Macbeth to kill anyone and that Banquo hears similar promises without acting on them. Readers also argue about how responsible Lady Macbeth is, and whether Macbeth keeps any of the audience's sympathy as his crimes grow."
    ],
