  {
    "title": "King Lear: Love, Power and Suffering",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Shakespearean tragedy",
    "passage": [
      "Shakespeare's King Lear (about 1606) begins with the aging Lear dividing his kingdom among his three daughters according to how much each says she loves him. Goneril and Regan give extravagant speeches. Cordelia, the youngest, says she loves him according to her bond, no more nor less, and that she has nothing to say that would outdo her sisters. Lear disowns her and banishes the Earl of Kent, who defends her.",
      "Lear means to keep the name and honors of a king while giving away the power. Goneril and Regan soon cut his retinue and shut him out in a storm. On the heath, accompanied by his Fool and the disguised Kent, Lear goes mad, and begins to think about the poor wretches who have no shelter, saying he has taken too little care of this.",
      "A parallel plot follows the Earl of Gloucester. His illegitimate son Edmund, resenting his status, deceives him into believing his legitimate son Edgar is plotting against him. Edgar flees disguised as a mad beggar. Gloucester is later blinded by Regan's husband Cornwall for helping Lear, and says that as flies to wanton boys are we to the gods; they kill us for their sport.",
      "Cordelia returns with a French army and is reconciled with her father, who asks her forgiveness. They are defeated and captured. Edmund orders their deaths and repents too late. Cordelia is hanged, and Lear enters carrying her body and dies, perhaps believing she still breathes. Goneril poisons Regan and kills herself.",
      "For about a century and a half the play was usually staged in Nahum Tate's rewritten version, in which Cordelia lives and marries Edgar, because the original ending seemed unbearable. Critics still argue whether the play shows any justice or meaning in the universe, whether Cordelia's refusal to flatter was integrity or pride, and whether Lear's suffering redeems him or only destroys him."
    ],
