  {
    "title": "Lord of the Flies: Civilization and Savagery",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "English novel",
    "passage": [
      "William Golding's Lord of the Flies (1954) tells of a group of British schoolboys stranded on an uninhabited tropical island after their plane is shot down during a war. With no adults, they elect Ralph as chief. He uses a conch shell to call meetings, and whoever holds it may speak. The clever, asthmatic Piggy, whom the others mock, advises him.",
      "Ralph insists on building shelters and keeping a signal fire burning for rescue. Jack, leader of a former choir, takes charge of hunting. He grows obsessed with killing pigs, paints his face, and lets the signal fire go out at the moment a ship passes. The boys grow afraid of a beast they believe lives on the island.",
      "Jack breaks away and forms his own tribe, which most boys join for meat, feasting and ritual dancing. The quiet, spiritual Simon has a vision in which a pig's head on a stick, the Lord of the Flies, seems to tell him that the beast is part of the boys themselves. He discovers that the beast on the mountain is a dead parachutist. Running to tell the others, he is mistaken for the beast during a frenzied dance and killed.",
      "Jack's tribe steals Piggy's glasses, needed to make fire. When Ralph and Piggy go to demand them back, Roger, who has grown increasingly cruel, rolls a boulder that kills Piggy and shatters the conch. The tribe hunts Ralph and sets the island on fire. Running onto the beach, Ralph meets a naval officer who has seen the smoke. The boys weep, and the officer, embarrassed, turns to look at his warship.",
      "Golding said the book traces the defects of society back to the defects of human nature. Critics debate whether it shows that humans are naturally savage once rules disappear, or whether it reflects the particular boys, schooling and wartime culture Golding portrays. Some point to real cases of stranded children who cooperated. The ending's warship suggests the adult world may be no different."
    ],
