  {
    "title": "Kafka's The Metamorphosis: Alienation and Family",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Modernist fiction",
    "passage": [
      "Franz Kafka's novella The Metamorphosis (1915) begins as Gregor Samsa, a traveling salesman, wakes to find himself transformed into a monstrous insect-like creature. Kafka never explains the change. Gregor's first worries are practical: he has missed his train and fears losing the job with which he supports his parents and his sister Grete and pays off his father's debt to his employer.",
      "When the chief clerk arrives to demand an explanation and Gregor emerges, the clerk flees and his father drives him back into his room. Grete at first cares for him, bringing food and clearing furniture so he can crawl. His mother faints at the sight of him. His father, revealed to have saved some money all along, takes a job and wears his uniform even at home; at one point he pelts Gregor with apples, and one lodges in his back and festers.",
      "The family take in lodgers and hide Gregor. When Grete plays the violin for them, Gregor is drawn out by the music and wonders whether an animal could be so moved by it. The lodgers see him and give notice. Grete declares that the creature must go: if it were really Gregor, he would have left of his own accord. Gregor dies that night, thinking of his family with tenderness.",
      "The family feel relief. They take a day off, ride a tram into the country, and the parents notice that Grete has grown into a lovely young woman and think of finding her a husband. The story ends with her stretching her young body.",
      "Readers interpret the transformation in many ways: as an image of alienated labor that reduces a person to his usefulness, of illness or disability and how families treat the dependent, of Kafka's own relationship with a domineering father, or of an absurd universe without explanation. Kafka asked his publisher not to depict the insect on the cover. Critics also disagree about the family: whether they are cruel, or ordinary people overwhelmed by an impossible burden."
