      "The novel was long taught as a straightforward story of moral courage. More recent critics argue that it centers a white hero, gives Tom Robinson little inner life, and treats racism as the vice of poor whites like the Ewells while sparing the respectable town. Others defend it as a child's limited view that the reader is meant to see beyond. The publication of Go Set a Watchman in 2015, an earlier draft in which an older Atticus holds segregationist views, renewed the debate about his character."
